+## C-1 — `upgradeTo` has no access control
+
+**Severity:** Critical
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket.upgradeTo` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:84-89`)
+
+```solidity
+function upgradeTo(address newImplementation) external {
+    assembly { sstore(IMPLEMENTATION_SLOT, newImplementation) }
+    emit Upgraded(newImplementation);
+}
+```
+
+**What an attacker gets:** everything. There is no `onlyOwner`. Any address calls
+`MarketProxy.upgradeTo(attackerContract)`, which delegatecalls into the market and
+writes the ERC-1967 implementation slot. The next call routes to attacker code
+running in the proxy's storage context. They rewrite `owner`, call
+`vault.seize(...)` for every depositor, and transfer out the treasury's seeded
+USDC. One transaction, no preconditions, no capital required. This is drainable
+from the moment of deployment.
+
+**Fix:** add `onlyOwner`. Do not hand-roll this — inherit OpenZeppelin's
+`UUPSUpgradeable` and implement `_authorizeUpgrade` with the owner check, so you
+also get the `upgradeToAndCall` and proxiable-UUID protections. Put the owner
+behind a multisig plus a timelock before mainnet money is involved.
+
+---
+
+## C-2 — `setOracle` has no access control
+
+**Severity:** Critical
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket.setOracle` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:91-94`)
+
+**What an attacker gets:** the whole USDC float. Anyone points `oracle` at a
+contract they wrote. Then either:
+
+- report wstETH at an absurd price, deposit 1 wei of wstETH, and `borrow()` the
+  entire USDC balance — `healthFactor` passes because collateral value is fake; or
+- report wstETH at ~0, making every real position underwater, and call
+  `liquidateAll()` to seize all depositor collateral at a 5% bonus.
+
+**Fix:** `onlyOwner`, and a timelock on oracle changes specifically — an oracle
+swap is the single highest-leverage parameter in the system. Emit the old address
+alongside the new one so it is monitorable.
+
+---
+
+## C-3 — `setLiquidationThreshold` has no access control
+
+**Severity:** Critical
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket.setLiquidationThreshold`
+(`src/LendingMarket.sol:96-99`)
+
+**What an attacker gets:** the same two outcomes as C-2 without needing a fake
+oracle. Set the threshold to `type(uint256).max` and `healthFactor` overflows or
+returns an enormous value, so borrow limits vanish and the attacker drains USDC
+against dust collateral. Set it to `0` and every position with debt has
+`healthFactor == 0`, so `liquidateAll()` seizes the entire book.
+
+Note the contrast with `setBorrowRate` on line 101, which *does* have `onlyOwner`.
+The modifier was simply omitted on three of four setters — worth checking how that
+happened, because it suggests the access-control layer was never reviewed as a
+unit.
+
+**Fix:** `onlyOwner` on all four setters. Add sanity bounds (e.g. threshold in
+`[5000, 9500]` bps, bonus in `[0, 2000]` bps) so a fat-fingered governance call
+cannot brick the market either. Re-audit every `external` function for a missing
+modifier, not just these three.
+
+---
+
+## C-4 — `healthFactor` divides before multiplying; solvent users are liquidatable
+
+**Severity:** Critical
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket.healthFactor` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:146-151`)
+
+```solidity
+return (collateralValueUsd(user) / debtUsd) * liquidationThreshold * WAD / BPS;
+```
+
+Integer division happens **first**, so `collateralValue / debtUsd` collapses to a
+whole number before anything else runs. The health factor is therefore
+`floor(C/D) * 0.8e18`, quantised in steps of 0.8.
+
+Working it through: healthy requires `floor(C/D) * 0.8 >= 1`, i.e.
+`floor(C/D) >= 2`, i.e. **C/D >= 2.0**.
+
+**What an unlucky user actually gets:**
+
+- The advertised 8000 bps threshold (125% collateralisation) is fiction. The real
+  maximum LTV is **50%**. A user with $10,000 of wstETH who borrows $5,001 has
+  `_borrow` revert with `Unhealthy` — at 50.01% LTV, nowhere near the stated limit.
+- Worse, a user who borrows exactly $5,000 against $10,000 is at `C/D == 2` and is
+  healthy *by one wei*. `accrueInterest` then advances `borrowIndex` on the very
+  next block. Debt becomes $5,000.0000x, `floor(C/D)` drops to `1`, health factor
+  becomes `0.8e18`, and the position is liquidatable. **A user at 200%
+  collateralisation gets fully liquidated within blocks of borrowing, purely from
+  normal interest accrual, with no price movement at all.** They lose their whole
+  position and pay the 5% bonus to whoever calls `liquidate` first.
+- There is no attacker precondition here. This fires on the happy path. Every
+  borrower who does not massively over-collateralise loses money.
+
+Also note the precision loss compounds: at C/D just under 2, the returned health
+factor is `0.8e18`, a 20%-underwater reading for a position that is 199%
+collateralised. Any monitoring or liquidation bot reads garbage.
+
+**Fix:** never divide before you multiply. Compute in the numerator and divide
+once at the end:
+
+```solidity
+return collateralValueUsd(user) * liquidationThreshold * WAD / (BPS * debtUsd);
+```
+
+That yields `HF = C * LT / D`, which is the standard formula and is continuous
+rather than quantised. Then add invariant tests asserting that health factor is
+monotonic in debt and that a position at exactly the threshold is not liquidatable.
+While you are in here, cache `collateralValueUsd` — the current code recomputes
+the full oracle loop inside `liquidateAll`'s loop.
+
