+## H-1 — Oracle accepts stale, zero, and deprecated price data
+
+**Severity:** High
+**Contract / function:** `PriceOracle.getPrice` (`src/PriceOracle.sol:39-45`)
+
+```solidity
+int256 answer = feed.latestAnswer();
+return uint256(answer) * 10 ** (18 - feed.decimals());
+```
+
+`latestAnswer()` is Chainlink's **deprecated** API. It returns only the price —
+no `updatedAt`, no `roundId`, no `answeredInRound` — so there is no way to tell a
+live price from one that stopped updating days ago. The interface already declares
+`latestRoundData()`; it is simply not used.
+
+**What an unlucky user actually gets:**
+
+- **Stale price.** If the wstETH feed halts (aggregator issue, or the feed's
+  heartbeat lapses during volatility) the market keeps pricing collateral at the
+  last value. If the real price fell, borrowers extract USDC against collateral
+  that no longer covers it and the protocol eats the bad debt. If the real price
+  rose, users are liquidated against a price that no longer exists.
+- **Zero price on the debt asset is catastrophic.** If the USDC/USD feed returns
+  `0`, then `debtValueUsd == 0`, so `healthFactor` hits the `debtUsd == 0` branch
+  on line 148 and returns `type(uint256).max`. Every borrower becomes infinitely
+  healthy and can drain the entire USDC balance with zero collateral. There is no
+  zero check anywhere.
+- **Zero price on a collateral asset** reverts `_liquidate` with a division by
+  zero at line 229 (`takeUsd * unit / price`), so liquidations stop working
+  exactly when you need them.
+- **Negative answers** revert rather than inflate — `uint256(-1) * 1e10`
+  overflows under 0.8 checked arithmetic. That is a market-wide DoS, not a theft,
+  but it is still an uncontrolled failure mode.
+
+**Fix:** switch to `latestRoundData()` and validate every field:
+
+```solidity
+(uint80 roundId, int256 answer, , uint256 updatedAt, uint80 answeredInRound) = feed.latestRoundData();
+if (answer <= 0) revert BadPrice(token);
+if (updatedAt == 0 || block.timestamp - updatedAt > heartbeat[token]) revert StalePrice(token);
+if (answeredInRound < roundId) revert StalePrice(token);
+```
+
+Store a per-token `heartbeat` (the wstETH and USDC feeds on Arbitrum have
+different heartbeats — set each from the feed's published spec, not one global
+constant). Also validate `feed.decimals() <= 18` in `setFeed` rather than letting
+the exponent underflow at read time.
+
+---
+
+## H-2 — No Arbitrum sequencer-uptime check
+
+**Severity:** High
+**Contract / function:** `PriceOracle.getPrice` (`src/PriceOracle.sol:39-45`)
+
+The README pins deployment to Arbitrum One. Chainlink's L2 guidance is explicit:
+on an optimistic rollup you must consult the L2 Sequencer Uptime Feed before
+trusting a price, because feeds do not update while the sequencer is down.
+
+**What an unlucky user actually gets:** when the Arbitrum sequencer comes back
+from an outage, prices jump discontinuously to catch up with the market, and a
+backlog of transactions lands at once. Users had no ability to add collateral or
+repay during the outage. Liquidators — who can submit through the delayed inbox —
+sweep the book at the stale-then-suddenly-correct price. Every position that moved
+against its owner during the downtime is liquidated at a 5% bonus with no chance
+to defend. Arbitrum has had multi-hour sequencer outages; this is an operational
+certainty over a long enough deployment, not a hypothetical.
+
+`liquidateAll()` makes this materially worse: one keeper transaction sweeps the
+entire book the instant the grace period would have protected users.
+
+**Fix:** read the Arbitrum sequencer uptime feed
+(`0xFdB631F5EE196F0ed6FAa767959853A9F217697D`) in `getPrice`, revert if the
+sequencer is reported down, and require a grace period (3600s is the common
+choice) after it comes back up before prices are considered valid:
+
+```solidity
+(, int256 up, uint256 startedAt, , ) = sequencerUptimeFeed.latestRoundData();
+if (up != 0) revert SequencerDown();
+if (block.timestamp - startedAt < GRACE_PERIOD) revert GracePeriodNotOver();
+```
+
+---
+
+## H-3 — wstETH is priced with the stETH/USD feed
+
+**Severity:** High
+**Contract / function:** oracle configuration; consumed by
+`LendingMarket.collateralValueUsd` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:132-139`) and
+`LendingMarket._liquidate` (`:224`)
+
+From the README:
+
+| First listed collateral | wstETH `0x5979D7b546E38E414F7E9822514be443A4800529` |
+| Feed set for wstETH | stETH / USD `0x07C5b924399cc23c24a95c8743DE4006a32b7f2a` |
+
+**These are different assets.** stETH is rebasing and tracks ~1 ETH. wstETH is the
+non-rebasing wrapper; one wstETH is worth `stETH_per_wstETH` stETH, a ratio that
+starts at 1.0 and only grows as staking rewards accrue. It is currently around
+1.2 and rises every day. `getPrice` returns 1e18-scaled USD "per whole unit of
+token", and for wstETH that unit is worth ~20% more than the feed reports.
+
+**What an unlucky user actually gets:** every wstETH depositor has their
+collateral systematically undervalued by the full accumulated exchange rate. Their
+borrowing power is ~20% short of what the parameters promise, and — the part that
+costs real money — their health factor is computed ~20% low, so they are
+liquidated while genuinely 20% further from the threshold than the contract
+believes. Combined with C-4's quantisation, a user who thinks they are at a safe
+LTV is liquidated at a comfortable one. The error grows monotonically with time,
+so this gets worse every day the market is live and never self-corrects.
+
+The direction happens to be conservative for protocol solvency, which is why it
+would survive casual testing — the shortfall lands entirely on users.
+
+Note the same class of bug is waiting in the shortlist: **weETH and ezETH are also
+value-accruing wrappers**, and listing either against a plain ETH/USD or
+LST/USD feed reproduces this. If the ratio is ever applied in the wrong
+direction, the sign flips and it becomes free money for the borrower instead.
+
+**Fix:** price wstETH as `wstETH/stETH exchange rate × stETH/USD`, reading the
+rate from `IWstETH.stEthPerToken()` (or Chainlink's wstETH/stETH exchange-rate
+feed) — or use a direct wstETH/USD feed if one is available on Arbitrum One at
+deployment. Whichever you pick, the general rule to encode in `PriceOracle` is
+that the feed must price the *exact* token address being valued; add a
+deployment check that asserts a known price for each listed collateral within a
+tolerance band before the market accepts deposits.
+
