+
+## H-4 — `CollateralVault.withdraw` transfers before updating state
+
+**Severity:** High
+**Contract / function:** `CollateralVault.withdraw` (`src/CollateralVault.sol:44-53`)
+
+```solidity
+uint256 balance = balanceOf[msg.sender][token];
+if (balance < amount) revert InsufficientBalance();
+
+IERC20(token).transfer(msg.sender, amount);          // <-- external call
+balanceOf[msg.sender][token] = balance - amount;     // <-- state update after
+ILendingMarket(market).requireHealthy(msg.sender);   // <-- check after
+```
+
+Textbook checks-effects-interactions violation, twice over: the balance is written
+after the transfer, **and** the solvency check runs after the funds have left. The
+`balance` local is also read before the call, so a reentrant withdraw sees the old
+value and the final write clobbers the intermediate one.
+
+**What an attacker gets:** with any collateral token that yields control during
+`transfer` — ERC-777 with a `tokensReceived` hook, an ERC-677/`transferAndCall`
+token, or any token with a callback or upgradeable transfer path — the attacker
+reenters `withdraw` and drains the vault of every user's balance. Even without a
+callback token, the ordering means a borrower's collateral is out the door before
+`requireHealthy` runs; the health check only reverts the whole transaction, so it
+holds today, but it is protecting nothing structurally.
+
+wstETH has no transfer hook, so this is not exploitable at launch. It becomes
+exploitable the moment governance lists a token that does — and the README says
+the DAO has asked for a path to yield-bearing and rebasing collateral, which is
+exactly the token class where hooks show up. `listCollateral` is a single
+governance call with no review gate.
+
+**Fix:** reorder to checks-effects-interactions and add a reentrancy guard:
+
+```solidity
+balanceOf[msg.sender][token] = balance - amount;
+ILendingMarket(market).requireHealthy(msg.sender);
+IERC20(token).safeTransfer(msg.sender, amount);
+```
+
+Add `nonReentrant` to `deposit`, `withdraw` and `seize`, and use
+`SafeERC20.safeTransfer` (see M-5). Apply the same ordering review to
+`_liquidate`, which transfers collateral to `msg.sender` inside a loop.
+
+---
+
+## M-1 — `borrow(0)` permanently bricks `liquidateAll`
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket._borrow` (`:200`) and `liquidateAll` (`:190-195`)
+
+```solidity
+if (principalOf[borrower] == 0) borrowers.push(borrower);
+principalOf[borrower] += amount * WAD / borrowIndex;
+```
+
+With `amount == 0`: `principalOf` stays `0`, so the guard passes again on the next
+call. There is no minimum borrow, no zero check, and `borrowers` is never pruned.
+
+**What an attacker gets:** a cheap, permanent denial of service on the keeper's
+liquidation path. A single address calls `borrow(0)` in a loop — on Arbitrum, at
+L2 gas prices, tens of thousands of pushes cost very little — and `borrowers`
+grows without bound. `liquidateAll()` iterates the whole array calling
+`healthFactor` (which itself loops every collateral token and makes two external
+oracle calls per token) on each entry, so it exceeds the block gas limit and
+reverts forever. The array can never shrink. The README says `liquidateAll` is the
+keeper's tool "after a large price move" — precisely the moment it will be gone.
+
+`liquidate(user)` still works one at a time, so this is a Medium rather than a
+Critical, but it removes the mass-liquidation defence and the protocol absorbs
+bad debt during the exact scenario the function exists for. The same array also
+accumulates duplicates: after a liquidation zeroes `principalOf`, the next borrow
+pushes the address again.
+
+**Fix:** require `amount > 0` (and ideally a `minBorrow` dust floor). Drop the
+`borrowers` array from the contract entirely — indexing borrowers on-chain is the
+wrong layer. Have the keeper build the candidate list off-chain from `Borrowed`
+events and call a batch `liquidate(address[] calldata users)` that skips healthy
+entries. If you keep an on-chain array, it needs swap-and-pop removal on full
+repayment and liquidation, plus a bounded `liquidateRange(uint256 from, uint256 to)`.
+
