+
+## M-2 — No close factor: every liquidation is a 100% liquidation
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket._liquidate` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:209-234`)
+
+```solidity
+uint256 debt = debtOf(user);
+debtAsset.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), debt);
+principalOf[user] = 0;
+```
+
+The liquidator always repays the entire debt and seizes `debt × 1.05` worth of
+collateral. Comparable markets cap a single liquidation at 50% of the position
+(Aave's close factor) so a marginally unhealthy position is nudged back to health
+rather than closed out.
+
+**What an unlucky user actually gets:** a borrower who dips one wei below the
+threshold loses their whole position and pays a 5% penalty on the full debt, not
+on the amount needed to restore health. Given C-4, "one wei below the threshold"
+is where every borrower spends most of their time. A user who is 199%
+collateralised on a position the protocol claims to support at 125% is closed out
+entirely.
+
+**Fix:** add a close factor — cap `debt` at `min(debt, debtOf(user) * closeFactor / BPS)`
+with `closeFactor` at 5000 bps, and only allow a full close when the position is
+below a hard "bad debt" threshold (health factor under ~0.95) where partial
+liquidation cannot restore it. Re-check health after a partial liquidation.
+
+---
+
+## M-3 — Liquidators can be forced to absorb bad debt
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket._liquidate` (`:209-234`), `liquidateAll` (`:190-195`)
+
+`_liquidate` pulls the full debt from the liquidator up front, then seizes
+whatever collateral exists, capped at what the user actually has (line 227,
+`takeUsd = valueUsd < seizeUsd ? valueUsd : seizeUsd`). If the position is
+underwater past the bonus, the liquidator pays full debt and receives less than
+they paid. There is no check that the seizure covers the repayment, and no minimum
+profitability guard.
+
+**What an unlucky user gets:** a keeper calling `liquidateAll()` after a sharp
+move processes *every* underwater position in one transaction, including the ones
+that are net-negative, with no ability to skip them. They pay full debt on each
+and eat the shortfall. There is also no slippage parameter, so a price move
+between transaction submission and inclusion can turn a profitable liquidation
+into a loss — and `liquidateAll` has no way to express "only if profitable".
+
+The mirror of this: because `liquidateAll` reverts entirely if any single
+`_liquidate` reverts (a failed `transferFrom`, a zero price per H-1), one bad
+position blocks the sweep of all the good ones.
+
+**Fix:** compute the seizure first, require it covers the repayment plus a
+liquidator-supplied `minProfit`, and revert otherwise. Handle genuinely
+insolvent positions through an explicit socialised-bad-debt path (write the
+residual off against a reserve) rather than silently taxing whoever calls. In the
+batch path, wrap each `_liquidate` in `try`/`catch` so one failure does not abort
+the sweep.
+
+---
+
+## M-4 — Interest is derived from `block.number × 12` on Arbitrum
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket.accrueInterest` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:117-126`)
+
+```solidity
+uint256 secondsElapsed = blocksElapsed * SECONDS_PER_BLOCK;  // 12
+```
+
+The README says the index "advances by the elapsed time since the last accrual",
+but the code advances by an estimate derived from block numbers. On Arbitrum
+Nitro, `block.number` is **not** the L2 block height — it returns an approximation
+of the L1 block number, which updates on a lag and can jump by more than one.
+`block.timestamp` is available and is the direct measurement.
+
+**What this costs:** the borrow index drifts against wall-clock time in whichever
+direction the L1 estimate happens to lag or lead. Under-accrual is a direct
+revenue loss to the treasury (which the README says takes the interest);
+over-accrual charges borrowers interest they do not owe and, given C-4's
+quantisation, pushes them over the liquidation cliff early. The 12-second constant
+is also an L1 assumption baked into an L2 contract — it silently breaks if
+Ethereum's slot time ever changes, and it is already wrong across missed slots.
+
+Additionally, the accrual is linear within each interval rather than compounded,
+so the effective rate diverges from the advertised 400 bps APY depending on how
+often anyone touches the contract. That is a design choice, but it should be a
+documented one.
+
+**Fix:** store `lastAccrualTime` (a `uint40` timestamp) instead of
+`lastAccrualBlock` and use `block.timestamp - lastAccrualTime` directly. Delete
+`SECONDS_PER_BLOCK`. Since storage is append-only behind the proxy and nothing is
+deployed yet, make this change before the first deployment rather than adding a
+parallel slot later. If you want true compounding, use a binomial or exponential
+approximation rather than linear-per-touch.
+
+---
+
+## M-5 — ERC-20 return values are never checked
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Contract / function:** every transfer site — `LendingMarket._borrow` (`:204`),
+`repay` (`:177`), `_liquidate` (`:213`), `CollateralVault.deposit` (`:38`),
+`withdraw` (`:48`), `seize` (`:57`)
+
+Every call is a raw `IERC20(...).transfer(...)` / `transferFrom(...)` with the
+boolean discarded.
+
+**What this costs:** two distinct failure modes.
+
+1. **Tokens that return nothing.** `IERC20.transfer` is declared to return
+   `bool`, so Solidity inserts a return-data decode that reverts when the token
+   returns an empty buffer. **USDT is on the README's shortlist for the next
+   listing round** and is exactly this token: every `deposit`, `withdraw` and
+   `seize` for USDT collateral reverts, so the token is un-listable and, if
+   listed, funds deposited through some other path are frozen.
+2. **Tokens that return `false` instead of reverting.** The transfer silently
+   fails while the accounting proceeds as though it succeeded — in
+   `CollateralVault.deposit` that credits a user collateral they never delivered,
+   which is a direct mint of borrowing power.
+
+Arbitrum-native USDC and wstETH both revert on failure, so neither fires at
+launch. Both fire on the roadmap.
+
+**Fix:** use OpenZeppelin `SafeERC20` (`safeTransfer` / `safeTransferFrom`)
+everywhere. This is a mechanical change across six call sites.
+
+---
+
+## M-6 — Fee-on-transfer and rebasing collateral are silently mis-accounted
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Contract / function:** `CollateralVault.deposit` (`src/CollateralVault.sol:35-42`)
+
+```solidity
+IERC20(token).transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
+balanceOf[msg.sender][token] += amount;
+```
+
+The vault credits the *requested* amount, not the amount actually received.
+
+**What an attacker gets, per token class:**
+
+- **Fee-on-transfer:** the vault receives `amount - fee` but credits `amount`.
+  Every deposit inflates the accounting relative to real holdings. The last
+  users to withdraw find the vault short, and a borrower can borrow against
+  collateral that partly does not exist.
+- **Rebasing (the README says the DAO has asked for a path to rebasing
+  collateral — stETH is the obvious candidate):** the vault's real balance moves
+  while `balanceOf` stays fixed. Positive rebases strand yield in the contract
+  with no owner; a negative rebase (a slashing event) leaves recorded balances
+  exceeding real holdings, so withdrawals succeed until the vault runs dry and
+  the last users get nothing. Per-user accounting against an absolute token
+  amount cannot represent a rebasing asset at all.
+
+**Fix:** measure the delta — snapshot `IERC20(token).balanceOf(address(this))`
+before and after the transfer and credit the difference. That handles
+fee-on-transfer. Rebasing tokens need share-based accounting (store shares, derive
+balances from the vault's total holdings), which is a real design change — until
+that exists, add an explicit deny at `listCollateral` and document that rebasing
+tokens are not supported. Do not let the DAO list one against the current vault.
+
+---
+
+## M-7 — No delisting path; one broken feed DoSes the entire market
+
+**Severity:** Medium
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket.listCollateral` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:107-114`),
+`collateralValueUsd` (`:132-139`)
+
+`listCollateral` only ever adds. There is no `delistCollateral`, nothing removes
+from the `collateralTokens` array, and `vault.setSupported(token, false)` is
+unreachable because no function calls it with `false`. Note also that
+`isCollateral` is written but never read anywhere — dead state that suggests the
+delisting logic was planned and dropped.
+
+**What this costs:** `collateralValueUsd` loops every listed token and calls
+`oracle.getPrice(token)` and `IERC20(token).decimals()` on each. If any single
+listed token's feed reverts — deprecated aggregator, feed unset during a
+migration, the H-1 negative-price overflow, or a token that self-destructs its
+`decimals()` — then `healthFactor` reverts for *every user*, which means `borrow`,
+`repay`, `liquidate`, `liquidateAll` and `CollateralVault.withdraw` all revert.
+The entire market freezes with every position stuck, and governance has no lever
+to remove the offending token. Given the README plans to list weETH, ezETH and
+USDT next, the array grows and so does the blast radius.
+
+**Fix:** add `delistCollateral(address token) external onlyOwner` that removes the
+token from `collateralTokens` (swap-and-pop), clears `isCollateral`, and calls
+`vault.setSupported(token, false)` — with existing depositors still able to
+withdraw. Separately, make `collateralValueUsd` resilient: `try`/`catch` the
+oracle call and treat an unavailable price as a reason to block *borrowing*
+without blocking *repayment and withdrawal*. Read `isCollateral` in `deposit`'s
+path or delete it.
+
+---
+
+## L-1 — Repay rounding leaves permanent dust debt
+
+**Severity:** Low
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket.repay` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:170-181`)
+
+```solidity
+uint256 debt = debtOf(onBehalfOf);          // principal * index / WAD, rounds down
+if (amount > debt) amount = debt;
+principalOf[onBehalfOf] -= amount * WAD / borrowIndex;   // rounds down again
+```
+
+Both conversions truncate, so repaying the full reported `debt` reduces
+`principalOf` by slightly less than the stored principal. A borrower who repays
+everything is left with a few wei of principal that they can never clear — each
+subsequent attempt hits the same rounding. The residual then accrues interest
+forever.
+
+**What an unlucky user gets:** a position that cannot be closed. They keep a dust
+debt, `debtOf` never returns zero, and they remain subject to the health check on
+withdrawal in perpetuity. It is small in absolute terms, but it means the
+"I'm done, give me my collateral back" flow never completes cleanly, and it
+leaves stale entries in `borrowers` feeding M-1.
+
+**Fix:** add an explicit full-repayment path — if the caller passes
+`type(uint256).max`, or if the computed principal reduction is within a dust
+threshold of `principalOf`, set `principalOf[onBehalfOf] = 0` and pull exactly
+`debtOf`. Round *against* the user on the debt side and *for* the protocol on the
+principal side consistently, and document which way each conversion rounds.
+
+---
+
+## L-2 — Withdrawal health check runs against a stale borrow index
+
+**Severity:** Low
+**Contract / function:** `CollateralVault.withdraw` (`:50`) → `LendingMarket.requireHealthy` (`:153-155`)
+
+`requireHealthy` is `view`, so it cannot accrue, and `withdraw` does not call
+`accrueInterest()` first. The health check therefore uses whatever `borrowIndex`
+was left by the last state-changing call, understating debt by the interest
+accrued since.
+
+**What an attacker gets:** marginally more collateral out than they should. At 400
+bps the drift is small, and it is bounded by how long the market has gone
+untouched — but the README explicitly states that `accrueInterest()` runs "at the
+top of every state-changing entry point", and this is a path where it does not.
+The gap widens if the borrow rate is ever raised.
+
+**Fix:** have `CollateralVault.withdraw` call a non-view market entry point that
+accrues first — e.g. change `requireHealthy` to non-`view`, accrue, then check.
+Audit every other entry point against the README's stated invariant.
+
+---
+
+## L-3 — Implementation left uninitialized; initializer is unguarded
+
+**Severity:** Low
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket.initialize` (`src/LendingMarket.sol:56-82`)
+
+`initialize` is `external` with only an `initialized` flag. `MarketProxy`'s
+constructor delegatecalls `initData` atomically, so the proxy cannot be
+front-run *provided* it is deployed with non-empty `initData` — that is a
+deployment-script property, not a contract guarantee. If anyone ever deploys the
+proxy with empty `initData`, the initializer is a free ownership grab in the
+mempool.
+
+The implementation contract itself is left uninitialized, so anyone can call
+`initialize` on it directly and become owner of the implementation's own storage.
+That is harmless today only because the implementation holds no funds and has no
+`selfdestruct` (removed in Cancun) — but combined with C-1's unguarded
+`upgradeTo`, it is one more reason the upgrade path needs locking down properly.
+
+**Fix:** disable the initializer on the implementation in its constructor
+(OpenZeppelin's `_disableInitializers()`), use the `initializer` modifier rather
+than a hand-rolled bool, and add a deployment assertion that `owner != address(0)`
+immediately after the proxy is constructed.
+
+---
+
+## L-4 — No ownership transfer, no pause, no zero-address validation
+
+**Severity:** Low
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket` (contract-wide)
+
+`LendingMarket` has an `owner` and an `onlyOwner` modifier but **no way to change
+the owner** — no `transferOwnership`, no renounce. If the deployment key is lost
+or compromised, there is no rotation path; the only recovery is an upgrade, which
+itself requires the owner. `PriceOracle` has a transfer but it is single-step,
+so a typo sends ownership to an unrecoverable address.
+
+There is also no pause / emergency stop anywhere. When one of the findings above
+fires in production, there is no way to halt borrowing while a fix ships.
+
+`initialize` validates none of its four addresses; a zero `oracle` or `vault`
+bricks the market permanently, and a zero `owner` locks governance out forever.
+
+**Fix:** add two-step ownership transfer (`Ownable2Step`) to both `LendingMarket`
+and `PriceOracle`; add `Pausable` with `whenNotPaused` on `borrow`,
+`borrowWithSig` and `CollateralVault.deposit` (never on `repay`, `withdraw` or
+`liquidate` — users must always be able to get out); and add zero-address checks
+in `initialize` and every setter. Put the owner behind a multisig with a timelock
+on parameter changes.
+
+---
+
+## L-5 — Unbounded loop over `collateralTokens`
+
+**Severity:** Low
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket.collateralValueUsd` (`:132-139`), `_liquidate` (`:219-231`)
+
+Both loops iterate every listed collateral for every user, making two external
+calls per token (`oracle.getPrice` and `IERC20.decimals()`) regardless of whether
+the user holds any of it — the `amount == 0` continue happens *after* the vault
+read. `healthFactor` calls `collateralValueUsd`, and `liquidateAll` calls
+`healthFactor` per borrower, so cost is O(borrowers × tokens) in one transaction.
+
+With one collateral this is fine. As the README's listing roadmap plays out, gas
+on every borrow and withdraw grows linearly, and `liquidateAll` becomes
+unusable well before that (see M-1).
+
+**Fix:** cache `IERC20.decimals()` in a mapping at `listCollateral` time — it is
+immutable per token and does not need an external call per read. Track a
+per-user list of tokens they have actually deposited and iterate that instead of
+the global list. Cap the number of simultaneously listed collaterals.
+
+---
+
+## L-6 — Proxy upgrade has no codesize check and no atomic initialization
+
+**Severity:** Low
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket.upgradeTo` (`:84-89`), `MarketProxy` (`src/MarketProxy.sol`)
+
+Beyond the missing access control (C-1): `upgradeTo` does not verify that
+`newImplementation` contains code. Setting it to an EOA or an empty address makes
+every subsequent `delegatecall` succeed vacuously — `delegatecall` to an
+addressless target returns `true` with empty returndata — so the proxy silently
+accepts calls and does nothing, with no way to recover because the recovery path
+runs through the same broken proxy. There is also no `upgradeToAndCall`, so any
+upgrade needing migration logic must run it in a second transaction, leaving a
+window where new code sits on unmigrated storage.
+
+`MarketProxy` has no admin of its own and relies entirely on the implementation
+for upgrade authorization, so a future implementation that omits `upgradeTo`
+freezes the proxy permanently.
+
+**Fix:** adopt OpenZeppelin's UUPS implementation, which handles the codesize
+check, the proxiable-UUID check (preventing an upgrade to a non-upgradeable
+implementation), and `upgradeToAndCall`. Add a storage `__gap` to `LendingMarket`
+now, before deployment, so future versions have room without the append-only
+constraint becoming painful.
+
+---
+
+## I-1 — `domainSeparator` is cached at initialization
+
+**Severity:** Informational
+**Contract / function:** `LendingMarket.initialize` (`:71-79`), `borrowWithSig` (`:162-168`)
+
+`domainSeparator` is computed once and stored, capturing `block.chainid` at
+deployment. If Arbitrum One ever forks, signatures are valid on both chains
+simultaneously. Standard practice is to cache for the deployment chain but
+recompute when `block.chainid` differs from the cached value.
+
+**Fix:** store the original chain id and rebuild the separator on mismatch — or
+inherit OpenZeppelin's `EIP712`, which does this. Moot if you take the C-5
+recommendation and drop `borrowWithSig` for launch.
+
+---
+
+## I-2 — Deployment ordering: `CollateralVault.market` must be the proxy
+
+**Severity:** Informational
+**Contract / function:** `CollateralVault` constructor (`src/CollateralVault.sol:27-29`)
+
+`market` is `immutable` and set at construction, but the market lives behind
+`MarketProxy` — so `market_` must be the **proxy** address, not the
+implementation. Meanwhile `MarketProxy`'s constructor runs `initialize`, which
+takes the vault address. The two contracts reference each other at construction
+time and there is no deployment script in the repo to pin the ordering.
+
+If the vault is pointed at the implementation address by mistake, `setSupported`
+and `seize` revert with `NotMarket` forever, and because `market` is `immutable`
+there is no fix short of redeploying the vault and migrating every deposit.
+Nothing in the code catches this.
+
+**Fix:** deploy the proxy first with empty `initData`, then the vault pointing at
+the proxy, then call `initialize` through the proxy — but see L-3, that
+reintroduces the front-running window, so guard the initializer properly first.
+Alternatively use CREATE2 to precompute the proxy address. Either way, commit the
+deployment script to the repo and add a post-deployment assertion that
+`CollateralVault.market == address(proxy)` and that
+`LendingMarket.vault == address(vault)`.
+
