+## What I did not cover
+
+- **No tests were run.** The repo has no test suite and no dependencies, so
+  nothing here is confirmed by execution — findings are traced by hand from the
+  source. C-4 in particular deserves a numeric test before you act on it, and it
+  will be trivial to write: assert that a position at 60% LTV is healthy.
+- **No deployment or governance scripts exist** to review, so the operational
+  half of the threat model (key custody, multisig thresholds, timelock delays,
+  upgrade rehearsal) is unexamined. Given C-1 through C-3, that half matters as
+  much as the code.
+- **Economic / parameter modelling** is out of scope: whether 8000 bps and 500
+  bps are the right numbers for wstETH's volatility and Arbitrum's liquidation
+  latency is a risk-modelling question, not a code question.
+- **No formal verification or fuzzing.** The interest-index arithmetic
+  (`accrueInterest` / `debtOf` / `repay` rounding) is the natural target for an
+  invariant suite: sum of `debtOf` should never diverge from the index, and
+  repayment should always be able to reach zero.
+
+**Recommended sequence:** fix C-1 through C-3 (one-line changes, but re-audit all
+`external` functions for missing modifiers rather than patching only these
+three). Then rewrite `healthFactor` for C-4 and delete `borrowWithSig` for C-5.
+Then the oracle work — H-1, H-2 and H-3 are one focused piece of work on
+`PriceOracle` plus its configuration. Write the test suite against the fixed
+contracts, and get a second review of the liquidation path specifically before
+the two-week date. The two-week timeline does not look realistic for this
+volume of change plus a test suite that does not yet exist.

